CORE
main.c
--32 --unwind 5
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
pthread_mutex_lock(&c.mutex[j]), j nondeterministic, writes the lock state
through a pointer whose target is c at an undecided offset: a byte update of
the whole struct at a non-constant offset. It is lowered by turning c into
bytes, updating them, and extracting c back. The bytes are now a single array
literal, element j being offset == j ? value : old, with the value bound once
by a let, rather than a with over the bytes: extracting c from the with
indexed it once per byte and simplified it whole each time, and the value
appeared once per byte (goblint-regression/09-regions_19-nested_nr: Convert
SSA 54 s -> 1.9 s).

The lock must leave the slots unchanged, lock c.mutex[j] and no other.
Passes before and after the change; it fails if an element takes the value
regardless of the offset, or keeps its old value where the offset matches.
See byte_update_struct_symbolic_offset_locked and
byte_update_struct_symbolic_offset_other, which must stay FAILED.
